---
import AtlasLayout from '../../layouts/AtlasLayout.astro';
import VersionLabel from '../../components/VersionLabel.astro';
---

<AtlasLayout
  title="Changelog"
  description="Version history of the AI Evaluation Atlas."
  currentPath="changelog/"
>
  <header class="entry-header">
    <p class="kicker">Version history</p>
    <h1>Changelog</h1>
    <p class="summary">
      Material changes to the Atlas — new entries, reclassifications, and
      corrections — are recorded here. Current release: <VersionLabel />
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="guide-prose" style="margin-top: 1.8rem;">
    <h2 id="v0-2">Version 0.2 — August 3, 2026 · Editorial architecture</h2>
    <p>
      A depth release, not a breadth release: the Atlas's editorial voice
      and teaching apparatus, with no new benchmark entries.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Editorial Philosophy</strong> page added, in the navigation
        and footer: how the Atlas should be read — evidence-informed,
        transparent about interpretation, open to revision.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>How to Use This Atlas</strong> added, with a recommended
        reading order for newcomers and shortcuts by need.
      </li>
      <li>
        Homepage reoriented from browsing to learning: why the Atlas
        exists, who it is for, and a four-step learning path.
      </li>
      <li>
        Entry pages restructured around <strong>Evidence Boundaries</strong>
        (what evidence a method produces, claims it can and cannot support,
        when to combine it with other methods), with new
        <strong>Common Misconceptions</strong>, <strong>Ongoing
        Debate</strong>, and closing <strong>Questions to Ask</strong>
        sections, plus a "Why this matters" block on every page.
      </li>
      <li>
        New flagship guide: <em>Reading an AI Evaluation Report</em>, with a
        printable ten-point checklist; subsequent guides renumbered.
      </li>
      <li>
        Taxonomy reworked for whitespace and relationships between the
        three views; footer gains the Atlas's identity line.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="v0-1">Version 0.1 — July 31, 2026</h2>
    <p>Initial public release establishing the Atlas framework.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Site framework established as a standalone, statically generated
        Astro project, with all content in editable Markdown files validated
        at build time.
      </li>
      <li>
        Taxonomy defined: eight entry types, five evidence classes, twelve
        evaluation levels, nine lifecycle stages, and controlled vocabularies
        for purpose, evaluator, environment, scoring mechanism, and construct.
      </li>
      <li>Lifecycle map added, covering design through retirement.</li>
      <li>
        Initial entries created: six substantive method entries, plus
        structured draft stubs for named benchmarks, infrastructures,
        standards, observatories, and evaluation-science concepts.
      </li>
      <li>
        First two guides drafted (<em>What Counts as AI Evaluation?</em> and
        <em>Evaluation Is Measurement</em>); remaining guides published as
        structured outlines.
      </li>
      <li>
        Source-verification workflow added: separate source records with
        per-record verification status, and visible draft and
        needs-verification badges on entries.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</AtlasLayout>
